# Lab 7 – 3-Bit Gray Code Counter

## Overview
This lab focused on designing and implementing a 3-bit Gray code counter using D flip-flops and combinational logic. Unlike a standard binary counter, a Gray code counter changes only one bit between consecutive states.

The circuit was implemented on a breadboard using 74LS74 D flip-flops and logic gates. LEDs were used to display the current 3-bit state.

## Gray Code Sequence

The counter follows the repeating 8-state sequence:

```text
000 → 001 → 011 → 010 → 110 → 111 → 101 → 100 → 000
```

Each clock pulse advances the counter to the next state.

## Hardware Components

- 74LS74 D flip-flops
- Combinational logic gates
- Breadboard and jumper wires
- LEDs for displaying counter outputs
- Function generator for the clock signal
- Reset push button and resistors

## Circuit Operation

The circuit uses three D flip-flops to store the current state of the counter. Combinational logic determines the next state based on the current outputs.

All three flip-flops share a clock signal. On each rising clock edge, the flip-flops update their outputs to the next Gray code state.

A 1 Hz clock signal allows the transitions to be observed through the LEDs. The reset button initializes the counter to `000`.

